      "content": "Variables like low residential churn, high local job density, and consistent property tax assessments do sometimes correlate with more stable repayment histories, but these signals are often difficult to isolate from systemic socioeconomic bias without rigorous debiasing. Because neighborhood data can act as a proxy for protected classes, most regulators and risk teams now favor individual-digitized behaviors or time-series customer metrics over static zip-code characteristics to meet fairness mandates. It is often more effective to weight local economic indicators as soft signals only when explicitly validated against audited fairness tests across demographic slices.",
